    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for LawDog hinges on exploiting high-upside matchups against back-end rotation arms. The premier play is **Lawrence Butler** (73.47), who benefits from a massive 13% platoon boost and favorable park conditions against Littell. While his position in the 8-hole is a minor drag, his efficiency ceiling against a shaky starter makes him our highest-projected asset. Close behind is **Dylan Beavers** (64.32), whose elite sprint speed and aggressive base-stealing profile against Arrighetti capitalize on the \"Slow SP Delivery\" metric, providing a necessary boost to our scarcest category. **Bryce Harper** (62.46) remains a mandatory start despite facing the tough Manaea; the combination of a 0.391 xwOBA and favorable wind direction at his back provides the requisite floor to anchor the UTIL spot.\n\nThe most difficult benching decision involved **Jung Hoo Lee** (46.06), who narrowly missed the cut. While he offers a decent efficiency profile, he was pushed out by the surging numbers of **Gavin Sheets** (62.32) and **Yainer Diaz** (61.74), both of whom benefit from substantially better SP_xERA matchups. **JJ Bleday** (61.31) also presents an interesting case; despite a 14% penalty for platoon splits against Sugano, the massive 14% park factor and the high-value 3-hole lineup spot effectively canceled out the SP skill disadvantage, making him a stronger start than the bench alternatives like **Ryan Waldschmidt** (45.19) or the struggling **Drew Gilbert** (38.71).\n\nRisk management remains a priority given the volatility of today\u2019s matchups. While we are starting **Cal Raleigh** (52.21) and **Masyn Winn** (57.07), both face pitchers with sub-4.40 xERAs, signaling a tighter window for production. We continue to monitor the status of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who remains sidelined on the IL, leaving our infield depth dependent on the steady floor of **Geraldo Perdomo** (56.17) and **Luis Arraez** (50.37). Ensure all lineups are finalized before first pitch to confirm that the assumed batting orders for **Taylor Ward** and others hold firm, as any drop in the batting order could immediately shift these marginal-to-strong plays below our efficiency floor.",
